# Root decision brief: bounded Darwin chmod classification

Date: 2026-08-27. Read-only leaf investigation; no repository edits, commits,
canonical fixture changes, dependency installation, production/native builds,
private poe-code access, global environment changes, or full-suite execution.

## Decision

The two frozen b494675c failures are two strict GNU9.7/Darwin test failures
covering 17 concrete directory transitions. Their bounded replays reproduce the
previously documented Darwin public-chmod versus GNU-fchmodat status/effect
distinction. These are new inputs in that already documented mismatch class,
not the historical six inputs, not equivalent outcomes, and not newly proven
portable POSIX bugs or typed-VFS violations. The selected strict GNU profile
remains unmet. No production fix or new permission API is proposed.

Keep both original GNU-strict assertions and every original failed row red until
ROOT explicitly approves a profile migration. Approval could introduce separately
labeled GNU9.7/Darwin-strict, Node22.22.2/libuv1.51.0/Darwin25.4.0 RealFS, and
host-credential-independent MemoryFS observations. Do not change old expected
statuses/modes, discard cases, count the alternate profile as repaired GNU parity,
or retroactively adjust the b494 full-gate denominator. Retain member-group SGID
success and real EACCES/no-effect controls beside any approved profile. No blanket
SGID rejection, native fallback/addon, retry, postcheck/rollback, or new API is
justified by this investigation.

