## Primary provenance and classification

Read current AGENTS, original runtime-review reports, Faraday feasibility archive,
and `tests/commands/core-regression-stress/NORMATIVE_PROFILES.md` from1f2aa30.
Current normative document SHA256 matches1f2aa30 exactly:
`0252eda91265d2074a5983fbbff9a3eff642f06eccde1bc4e9956debd0feb6fb`.
All10 archived SGID manifest records independently authenticate. Historical six
u-s,g=s,o-t file/directory cases, with027 umask and requested02707, remain distinct
historical failures. Their0/0707 outcome is not substituted for today's retained
SUID outcomes0/04xxx.

Six official sources were freshly fetched; every content hash matches the old
primary-source record. URLs, hashes, line locations and bounded excerpts are in
primary-sources.json: POSIX.1-2024 XSH chmod, XCU chmod, Apple pinned UNIX03 wrapper,
libuv1.51.0 Unix fs, Node22.22.2 node_file.cc, GNU9.7 chmod.c.

Source chain: metadata/chmod.ts requests the calculated mode; RealFS index.ts:416
forwards it to native.chmod at421; Node calls uv_fs_chmod; libuv calls public chmod.
Apple's pinned public wrapper can retry EPERM requests without SGID. GNU9.7
chmod.c:307 calculates the mode and312 calls fchmodat. The historical archive
traced that same binary's fchmodat route and distinguished raw __chmod from public
chmod. Today's matched binary identity, direct Node/RealFS behavior, VFS request
trace and successful member controls support the SAME host-API distinction.
No new kernel/syscall interposition, signed shared-cache attestation, Linux run,
or exhaustive kernel-policy attribution is claimed.

POSIX permits implementation-defined set-ID restrictions, and gives directory
set-ID requests implementation latitude. Successful chmod updates ctime. POSIX
also specifies equivalent behavior for fchmodat(AT_FDCWD,path,mode,0) and chmod;
the documented divergent Darwin public paths are therefore an apparent API
conformance inconsistency, not a portable GNU semantic requirement. Successful
Node SGID clearing is not proved wrong by this GNU oracle. These directory cases
must not be justified solely by the separate mandatory regular-file rule.

Contract inspected: src/contracts/filesystem.md:235 promises POSIX-style supported
mode handling, not universal retention of requested special bits or GNU's precise
Darwin syscall path. src/fs/memory/index.ts:489 stores the requested virtual mode;
RealFS does not swallow a native error here because native.chmod fulfills.
The genuine EACCES control preserves typed meaning/path and no effects. No exact
typed-VFS contract violation is established, so there is no minimal production
fix requiring approval to propose. Existing strict product compatibility remains
unmet regardless of that conclusion.

